---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro';
import { organization } from '../config/organization';

const email = organization.contact.email;
const nmraStandards = 'https://www.nmra.org/index-nmra-standards-and-recommended-practices';

// NMRA RP-20.1: 1.5 oz plus 0.75 oz per inch of car body length.
const weights = [4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14].map((inches) => {
  const oz = 1.5 + 0.75 * inches;
  return { inches, oz, grams: Math.round(oz * 28.35) };
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="On3 Check Gauge | N.C.N.G. Model Railroad"
  description="How to use the club's printed On3 check gauge for track gauge, wheel spacing, coupler height, and trip pin height."
>
  <section class="page-container section">
    <SectionHeader
      as="h1"
      eyebrow="On3 check gauge"
      title="Using the printed On3 gauge"
      description="A small printed card that checks On3 track gauge, wheel back-to-back spacing, coupler height, and trip pin height."
    />

    <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-5 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <figure class="space-y-2">
        <img
          src="/images/on3-gauge/card-front.svg"
          alt="The gauge card standing across two rails. Bottom edge: a check-mark tab that drops between the rails and a notched cross tab that must not. Top edge: a check-mark finger and a notched cross finger for wheels. Middle: a pad with a Kadee coupler box under it, the letters ON3, and a QR code on the left. A low ledge sits between the rails."
          width="720"
          height="440"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg border border-gray-200"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600">The card, seen from the marked face.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The card uses symbols instead of instructions. Every check uses the same two:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="solar:check-circle-bold" class="w-6 h-6 shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-primary" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span><strong>Check mark:</strong> this part should fit.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="solar:close-circle-bold" class="w-6 h-6 shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-primary" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span>
            <strong>Cross, with a small V-notch cut in the part:</strong> this
            part should <em>not</em> fit. If it does, the track or wheels are too wide.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Tip: to make the marks easier to see, run the side of a permanent marker
        lightly across the face of the card. The ink catches only the raised marks.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">Track gauge</h2>
      <p>The two tabs on the bottom edge check the distance between the rails.</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>
          Stand the card across the track with the check-mark tab between the
          rails. It should drop in and the card should sit on both rail heads.
          If it will not drop in, the track is too narrow.
        </li>
        <li>
          Move the card so the cross tab is over the rails. It should rest on
          top of the rails. If it drops between them, the track is too wide.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Check curves, turnouts, and rail joints, where gauge most often drifts.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">Wheel spacing (back-to-back)</h2>
      <p>
        The two fingers on the top edge check the distance between the backs
        of the wheels on an axle.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>
          Turn the car upside down. Set the check-mark finger between the
          wheels on one axle. It should slip in. If it will not, the wheels
          are too close together.
        </li>
        <li>
          The cross finger should not slip between the wheels. If it does, the
          wheels are too far apart.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Use light pressure. Do not force either finger.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">Coupler and trip pin height</h2>
      <p>
        This check uses a real Kadee coupler as the reference. One-time setup:
        screw a Kadee #803 coupler box under the pad with the screw that comes
        in the pack, coupler pointing away from the card. Cut off that
        coupler's trip pin.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>
          On straight, level track, stand the card across the rails with the
          check-mark tab between them. Hold the top edge lightly.
        </li>
        <li>Roll the car in until its coupler closes with the card's coupler.</li>
        <li>
          Look from the side, with your eye at coupler height. The two knuckles
          should line up. If the car's knuckle sits higher or lower, the coupler
          needs adjusting.
        </li>
        <li>
          The car's trip pin should pass over the low ledge between the rails
          without touching it. If it drags, bend the pin up.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <figure class="space-y-2">
        <img
          src="/images/on3-gauge/coupler-side.svg"
          alt="Side view of the coupler check. The card stands on the rail with a Kadee coupler under its pad. The car's coupler closes with it at the same height, and the car's trip pin passes just over a low ledge on the rail."
          width="720"
          height="430"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg border border-gray-200"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600">Coupler check, seen from the side.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">Car weight</h2>
      <p>
        The NMRA recommends 1.5 ounces plus 0.75 ounce for each inch of car
        body length. Weigh the car on a kitchen scale.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full max-w-md text-left border-collapse">
          <caption class="sr-only">Recommended On3 car weight by body length</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-300">
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Body length</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Ounces</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-semibold">Grams</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {weights.map((w) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-gray-200">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">{w.inches} in</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">{w.oz} oz</td>
                <td class="py-2">{w.grams} g</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">What the card measures</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Track gauge: 19.05 mm minimum, 19.41 mm maximum (NMRA S-3.2).</li>
        <li>Wheel back-to-back: 16.92 mm minimum, 17.12 mm maximum (NMRA S-4.2).</li>
        <li>Coupler height: matches a Kadee #803 mounted at 5/8 in, which puts the knuckle center 9/16 in above the rail.</li>
        <li>Trip pin: at least 1/16 in above the rail.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The card does not check flange depth, the wheel check gauge, or
        turnout flangeways. Those tolerances are too tight for a printed part.
        Use a metal NMRA gauge for them.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a printed club tool, not a certified NMRA gauge. Printers vary
        by a tenth of a millimeter or more, so treat a borderline result as a
        reason to check again with a metal gauge or calipers. Read the full
        standards on the
        <a href={nmraStandards} class="text-primary underline">NMRA standards page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10">Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email
        <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class="text-primary underline">{email}</a>
        if something on the card is unclear or a check gives a result you do
        not expect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
